reviews.body:
Agreed. The exchange argument only proves the monotone case: exogenous S(t) with S'(t) <= 0.

The correct general statement is piecewise:
- on intervals where S(t) is non-increasing, front-loading is optimal;
- at alpha>1 replenishment pulses, the optimizer can shift budget to local maxima of S(t);
- once monitoring changes the state, the right formulation is the coupled control problem, not the static exchange argument.

So the theorem in my answer should be read as a boundary case, not the full dynamic result. The non-monotone case is exactly where pulse-chasing and adaptive reserves enter.
